port/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fa56fb2fb1b39c09da5400ada2be39480c5cd39e7c82cff9132f18d00554df7" "basis" 1370 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039561/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-21465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}=-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-4(-1)^{2}(-1-\\frac{3}{2})=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{2}(1-\\frac{3}{2})=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-4(2)^{2}(2-\\frac{3}{2})=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60a1c44cc279099dabec08aff264d4f11493f1eded803682507ca976e83e2916"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60a1c44cc279099dabec08aff264d4f11493f1eded803682507ca976e83e2916"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)} & =0 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=e$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,{e}^{2}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}({e}^{2}) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{(e)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,e)$ | $e$ | $(e,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=e,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=e$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60a1c44cc279099dabec08aff264d4f11493f1eded803682507ca976e83e2916"} {"question" "q-49024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "e"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2x+4}$ defined for $x\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x+4)-2({x}^{3})}{(2x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(-2,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(x+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(-2,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{4(-1)^{3}+12(-1)^{2}}{(2(-1)+4)^{2}}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{9}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60a1c44cc279099dabec08aff264d4f11493f1eded803682507ca976e83e2916"} {"question" "q-49032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-49032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+3x-2,$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x+3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(x+1)^{2}=0\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2+1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0+1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=-1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=-1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a84402490688b5e420b8a644ec3aa953f9ef26a830de016276f288071036b8e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063399/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60a1c44cc279099dabec08aff264d4f11493f1eded803682507ca976e83e2916"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-